explanation:
The following clinical and laboratory features suggest progression of acute hepatitis to chronic hepatitis:
• Lack of complete resolution of clinical symptoms of anorexia, weight loss, fatigue, and the persistence of hepatomegaly;
• The presence of bridging/interface or multilobular hepatic necrosis on liver biopsy during protracted, severe acute viral hepatitis;
• Failure of the serum aminotransferase, bilirubin, and globulin levels to return to normal within 6-12 months after the acute illness; and
• The persistence of HBeAg for >3 months or HBsAg for >6 months after acute hepatitis.
